---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import ExternalLink from '../../components/ExternalLink.astro';
import StatusBadge from '../../components/StatusBadge.astro';
import release from '../../data/release.json';
---

<PageLayout
  title="How it works"
  description="How Residiuum combines a mathematical query kernel, document-native data, exact ranked access designs, high-throughput indexed storage, and damage-local recovery."
  path="/how-it-works/"
  eyebrow="Mathematical architecture"
  lead="One exact semantic core beneath familiar developer surfaces, fast indexed storage, formal data rules, and recovery evidence."
>
  <Callout>
    <p>
      Every section links to documentation or repository specifications for
      normative detail. Maturity labels follow
      <a href="/status/">the public status contract</a>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The center: one deterministic kernel</h2>
  <p>
    Residiuum does not let each API, query dialect, rule engine, and recovery tool
    invent a slightly different meaning for values and predicates. RQL, its
    declared compatibility dialects, RRE, and examination reduce toward the
    SDA / ENR mathematical kernels.
  </p>
  <pre>{`SQL-ish / Mongo-ish / RQL        RRE rules
             \\                    /
              deterministic predicate + value semantics
                              ↓
                  indexed documents + evidence
                              ↓
             results / coverage / holes / provenance`}</pre>
  <p>
    The implemented RQL surface is currently a subset of the full v1 design.
    Raw SDA remains available when callers need the exact kernel directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Document-native application model</h2>
  <p>
    Applications open a store, name a collection, and put/get/delete values by
    string key (subject). The ordinary surface is the Rust
    <code>residiuum-sdk</code> collection API—not frames or segments.
  </p>
  <p>
    <ExternalLink href={`${release.docsBase}/`}>Docs home</ExternalLink>
    ·
    <ExternalLink href={release.github + '/blob/main/crates/residiuum-sdk/README.md'}>
      SDK README
    </ExternalLink>
  </p>

  <h2>Flexible documents without application-owned truth</h2>
  <p>
    Values may be JSON documents or opaque byte payloads. JSON gains filters,
    secondary indexes, and dialect query surfaces. Bytes remain first-class
    durable material without forced document interpretation.
  </p>
  <p>
    RRE is the normative design for finite, declarative document and transition
    invariants. Atomics is the enforcement model. Together they target a
    document database where the database—not an optional callback in application
    code—owns declared truth.
  </p>
  <p>
    <StatusBadge status="design" /> RRE and Atomics are specified direction,
    not release {release.productVersion} capabilities.
  </p>

  <h2>Direct rank instead of offset walking</h2>
  <p>
    Today, bounded resume-key cursors page subjects in stable order. Dingo
    Direct Access goes further: its normative design treats numeric position as
    an exact rank query. When admitted indexes contain enough counting
    information, the engine reaches result <code>k</code> without silently
    enumerating the preceding <code>k − 1</code> matches.
  </p>
  <p>
    <StatusBadge status="design" />
    <ExternalLink href={release.github + '/blob/main/DIRECT_ACCESS_SPEC.md'}>
      Dingo Direct Access specification
    </ExternalLink>
  </p>

  <h2>Sorting compiled into counted structure</h2>
  <p>
    Dingo Order Wavelets specify a derived rank/select structure for ordered
    fields. A filtered query transports its exact match bitmap through that
    structure and navigates conditional counts to the kth ordered answer. It
    avoids comparison-sorting every matching document at query time.
  </p>
  <pre>{`exact match bitmap
        +
versioned order wavelet
        +
rank/select branch counts
        =
exact kth filtered document in RQL order`}</pre>
  <p>
    <StatusBadge status="design" />
    <ExternalLink href={release.github + '/blob/main/ORDER_WAVELET_SPEC.md'}>
      Order Wavelet specification
    </ExternalLink>
  </p>

  <h2>A measured indexed engine</h2>
  <p>
    A current owner-observed local development run reports roughly
    <strong>350 MB/s</strong> of indexed ingest for 4 KiB writes across four
    workers on a MacBook Air M4 while the chaos agent punches storage holes.
    This is a diagnostic signal, not a cross-system benchmark or SLO.
  </p>
  <p>
    Comparative publication requires the full durability, dataset, verification,
    concurrency, revision, latency, and raw-artifact disclosure contract.
    <ExternalLink href={release.github + '/blob/main/doc/BENCHMARK_DISCLOSURE.md'}>
      Benchmark disclosure
    </ExternalLink>
  </p>

  <h2>Self-verifying storage units</h2>
  <p>
    Authoritative records are written as append-oriented, integrity-checked
    storage units. Verification is local to the unit: damage becomes an
    explicit hole rather than an invisible failure of the whole store.
  </p>
  <p>
    <ExternalLink href={release.github + '/blob/main/FORMAT_SPEC.md'}>
      FORMAT_SPEC.md
    </ExternalLink>
  </p>

  <h2>Derived indexes and catalogs</h2>
  <p>
    Primary and secondary indexes, catalogs, and other accelerators are
    rebuildable from authoritative segments. They speed reads and scans; they
    are not the only route back to surviving data.
  </p>

  <h2>History</h2>
  <p>
    Per-key history surfaces prior versions for inspection. History retention
    interacts with compaction and reclaim policies—see operator documentation
    for what is preserved versus reclaimed.
  </p>

  <h2>Query surfaces over the same semantics</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Query surfaces" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Surface</th>
          <th scope="col">Role</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Rust filters</td>
          <td>Typed collection filters in the SDK</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>RQL</td>
          <td>Official human query surface over collections</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dialects</td>
          <td>Optional SQL / JSON / mongo-style mimicry compiled to SDA</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Raw SDA</td>
          <td>Deterministic examination algebra for filters and recovery evidence</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <ExternalLink href={release.github + '/blob/main/doc/SDA/INTRODUCTION.html'}>
      SDA introduction
    </ExternalLink>
    ·
    <ExternalLink href={release.github + '/blob/main/doc/RQL/USER_GUIDE.md'}>
      RQL user guide
    </ExternalLink>
  </p>

  <h2>Durability acknowledgement modes</h2>
  <p>
    Writes acknowledge under explicit modes such as memory, buffered, and
    durable. Modes are not interchangeable in benchmarks or operational claims.
    Remote receipts require server-proved commit fields.
  </p>

  <h2>Recovery evidence and coverage</h2>
  <p>
    Salvage, scrub, and examination produce explicit evidence: verified data,
    partial data, holes, and coverage of what was searched. Incomplete tier or
    partition coverage must not look like empty success.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/survival/">Survival model</a>
    ·
    <ExternalLink href={release.docsRecovery}>Recovery guide</ExternalLink>
  </p>

  <h2>The important maturity boundary</h2>
  <p>
    <StatusBadge status="experimental" /> Embedded single-node is the strongest
    deployment path today. The language/storage kernel, recovery tools, and
    bounded cursors exist; RRE, full Direct Access, and Order Wavelets remain
    design work. Network multi-node remains experimental; single-node TCP is
    development-only. Details:
    <a href="/status/">status matrix</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="btn-row">
    <ExternalLink href={release.docsArchitecture} class="btn btn-primary">
      Explore specifications
    </ExternalLink>
    <a href="/status/" class="btn btn-secondary">See status</a>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
